  "slug": "acute-myeloid-leukemia-7857",
  "summary": "Spatial and single-cell transcriptomics now link malignant and immune cell states to tissue organization and clinical outcome.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is single-cell and spatial transcriptomics used to resolve malignant and immune cell states within tumor microenvironments and to relate those states to spatial organization and clinical behavior. In glioma, IDH status is associated with differences in malignant and immune architecture, spatially restricted iron-metabolism stress programs prime invasive mesenchymal glioblastoma states, and vascular heterogeneity remodels cell-cell communication in the blood-tumor barrier. Spatial omics in neuroblastoma resolves adrenergic and mesenchymal states, and multi-omic spatial profiling in head and neck adenoid cystic carcinoma defines outcome-associated tumor states. A point-process framework applied to CosMx glioblastoma data offers a hypothesis-driven route to infer interactions from spatial transcripts. Similar atlas approaches extend to non-malignant tissues, including fibrotic and inflammatory settings. These associations are descriptive and do not establish causal mechanisms.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-10"
}
